## Why

`jwt.verify()` throws on an expired token, and nothing catches it.

`src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17`:

```ts
const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);   // ← throws TokenExpiredError
const user = await this.userService.findById(decoded.id);
```

`jsonwebtoken` signals expiry by throwing `TokenExpiredError` (subclass of `JsonWebTokenError`) — it is **not** a Nest `HttpException`. Nest's default exception layer maps `HttpException` to its status and everything else to `500 Internal Server Error`. There's no global exception filter in this app (`main.ts` registers none), so the throw escapes untranslated.

Note the two explicit `throw new HttpException(..., UNAUTHORIZED)` branches on either side of that line (missing header → 401, unknown user id → 401) — which is exactly why it reads as if expiry is covered. It isn't: only the *absence* of a token is handled, never a *bad* one. Same bug for tampered and wrongly-signed tokens.

The expiry itself works fine — `generateJWT` sets `exp: exp.getTime() / 1000` (seconds, correct unit) at +60 days.

**There are two live call sites, not one.** `src/user/user.decorator.ts:15` has the identical unguarded `jwt.verify`, so public routes that take `@User('id')` (e.g. `GET /articles`, `GET /articles/:slug`) also 500 when the app sends an expired token. Fixing only the middleware leaves the app hitting 500s on the feed. (`src/shared/base.controller.ts:12` has it a third time, but nothing extends `BaseController` — dead code.)

This is `LESSONS.md` #79, still unfixed.

## The fix

Wrap both `jwt.verify` calls and translate to 401.

`src/user/auth.middleware.ts`:

```ts
let decoded: any;
try {
  decoded = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);
} catch (err) {
  throw new HttpException('Invalid or expired token.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
}
const user = await this.userService.findById(decoded.id);
```

`src/user/user.decorator.ts` (needs `HttpException, HttpStatus` added to the `@nestjs/common` import):

```ts
if (token && token[1]) {
  try {
    const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token[1], SECRET);
    return !!data ? decoded[data] : decoded.user;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new HttpException('Invalid or expired token.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
  }
}
```

One judgment call on the decorator: since it's *optional* auth, you could instead swallow the error and return `undefined` so public routes serve anonymous content. I'd argue against it — the mobile app would keep sending a dead token and never learn to log out, which is the failure this ticket is about. 401 on a bad token, anonymous only when no token is sent.

A global `HttpExceptionFilter` won't help here, by the way — the problem is the error class, not the rendering.

Want me to apply both edits? Heads up that this isn't a git repo, so there's no `git diff` to check my work against afterward.
